```cpp
if (hp <= 0) {
    std::cout << "Game over\n";
} else if (hp < 20) {
    std::cout << "Low health!\n";
} else {
    std::cout << "OK\n";
}
```

The condition goes in parentheses. Compare with `==`, `!=`, `<`, `<=`, `>` and `>=`, and combine conditions with `&&` (and), `||` (or) and `!` (not).

The branches are checked top to bottom, and only the **first** true one runs. A single `=` is assignment, not comparison: more on that trap below.

```cpp
while (hp > 0) {            // repeat while the condition holds
    hp -= 4;
}

for (int i = 0; i < 5; ++i) {   // start; keep going while; step
    std::cout << i << " ";      // 0 1 2 3 4
}
```

`++i` adds 1 to `i`. Inside any loop, `break` leaves the loop at once and `continue` skips straight to the next round. A variable declared inside braces, like `i` here, exists only inside them.

