steps.body:
Several functions can share a name if their **parameter types or counts** differ. The compiler picks the best match for the arguments, at compile time:

```cpp
void show(int x);
void show(double x);
void show(std::string s);
```

The return type alone isn't enough: `int load();` next to `double load();` fails with *functions that differ only in their return type cannot be overloaded*.

A parameter can have a **default value**, used when the caller leaves it out. Defaults go at the end of the list:

```cpp
int damage(int base, int mult = 2);
damage(5);      // mult is 2
damage(5, 3);   // mult is 3
```

