```cpp
#include <unordered_map>

std::unordered_map<std::string, int> price;
price["sword"] = 50;           // insert or overwrite
price["shield"] = 30;
price.at("sword");             // 50; throws if the key is missing
price.contains("bow");         // false (C++20)
price.size();                  // 2
price.erase("shield");         // remove a key

for (const auto& [name, cost] : price) {   // visit every pair
    std::cout << name << ": " << cost << "\n";
}
```

A `std::unordered_map<K, V>` is a hash table: looking up a key takes about the same time however many entries there are. The loop's order is **unspecified**, so never rely on it. If you need the keys in sorted order, `std::map` keeps them sorted (it's a tree, and a little slower).

