steps.body:
```cpp
class Player {
public:
    Player(std::string name, int maxHp)
        : name_(name), hp_(maxHp), maxHp_(maxHp) {}

    void takeDamage(int amount) {
        hp_ = std::max(0, hp_ - amount);
    }
    void heal(int amount) {
        hp_ = std::min(maxHp_, hp_ + amount);
    }
    int hp() const { return hp_; }

private:
    std::string name_;
    int hp_;
    int maxHp_;
};
```

`public:` members are the interface anyone can use; `private:` members can only be touched by the class's own member functions. This is **encapsulation**: no code outside can set `hp_` to -50 or 9999, so the rule "0 ≤ hp ≤ max" holds everywhere.

The **constructor** has the class's name and no return type, and runs when an object is created. The list after `:` sets each member. (`std::max` and `std::min` come from `<algorithm>`; the trailing `_` is just a common naming habit for private members.)

`struct` and `class` differ only in the default: members are public in a struct and private in a class.

