```cpp
enum class State { Idle, Running, Jumping };
```

```cpp
State s = State::Idle;
switch (s) {
    case State::Idle:    speed = 0; break;
    case State::Running: speed = 5; break;
    case State::Jumping: speed = 3; break;
}
```

An `enum class` is a type with a fixed set of named values. The names are scoped (`State::Idle`), and it doesn't silently convert to or from `int`, so a `State` can't be mixed up with a score.

`switch` jumps to the matching `case`. Each case needs a `break`, or execution **falls through** into the next one. With `-Wall`, clang warns when a switch over an enum misses a value: *enumeration value 'Jumping' not handled in switch*. Add a state later, and the compiler points out every switch to update.

