steps.body:
Some functions may have no answer: finding an item that isn't there, or reading a level name that doesn't exist. `std::optional<T>` (`#include <optional>`) holds either a `T` or nothing, written `std::nullopt`:

```cpp
std::optional<int> parseLevel(const std::string& s) {
    if (s == "easy") return 1;
    if (s == "hard") return 3;
    return std::nullopt;
}
```

Check before using it, with `if (opt)` or `opt.has_value()`, then read the value with `*opt`. Or say what to use instead: `opt.value_or(0)`. `opt.value()` throws `std::bad_optional_access` when it's empty.

It beats magic values like `-1`, which might be a real answer, and it's clearer than a pointer that may be null.

steps.answer:
```cpp
std::optional<std::size_t> findItem(const std::vector<std::string>& bag, const std::string& name) {
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < bag.size(); ++i) {
        if (bag[i] == name) return i;
    }
    return std::nullopt;
}

int main() {
    std::vector<std::string> bag = {"rope", "lamp", "key"};
    if (auto slot = findItem(bag, "key")) {
        std::cout << "key in slot " << *slot << "\n";
    }
    if (!findItem(bag, "map")) {
        std::cout << "no map\n";
    }
}
```

It prints `key in slot 2` and `no map`.

