steps.body:
`#include <algorithm>` gives you tested versions of common loops. They take a range as two **iterators**, `v.begin()` and `v.end()`, and many take a lambda:

```cpp
std::sort(v.begin(), v.end());                        // ascending
std::sort(v.begin(), v.end(), [](int a, int b) { return a > b; });  // descending
auto n = std::count_if(v.begin(), v.end(), [](int x) { return x < 0; });
auto it = std::find_if(v.begin(), v.end(), [](int x) { return x > 10; });
if (it != v.end()) { /* *it is the first match */ }
```

`find_if` returns an iterator, a position in the vector; `v.end()` means "not found". `*it` reads the element, and `it - v.begin()` is its index. C++20 also offers shorter versions such as `std::ranges::sort(v)`.

